            <ArticleTitle>[Low back pain with 'red flags': a case of spondylodiscitis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-72</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spondylodiscitis is a serious clinical entity. Despite the decrease in mortality from about 25% in the pre-antibiotic era to near 5%, it remains significant and the associated morbidity is still relevant. The rate of residual neurological deficits among survivors is around 7%. In 30% of patients some related symptoms persist, the most important being pain. The authors report the case of a 74-year-old male patient with recent onset low back pain, which caused considerable disability. With this work the authors intend to alert to the fact that in a patient with a common symptom such as low back pain, the presence of &quot;red flags&quot; requires a quick investigation and diagnosis in order to prevent serious damage.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
